A||N||7||FieroJoe||Crackly Speakers!||||4||
Z||000000||FieroJoe||03-24-2001||11:50 PM||||Alright, I have a question or two for your speaker/stereo guys out there.  I just got done installing my factory sub system.  The whole interior is taken apart, so I have easy access to all wires.  I dug up where I had put my head unit and my speakers, and reconnected them and just let them sit there (no dash) so I could hear how they sounded along with the sub.  Well, everything was great, until my 4x10's started cracking when the bass hit.  Almost sounded like they were blown, but they are brand new.  I disconnected the amp for the sub, thinking it may be some sort of feedback.  Nope, still sounding a tad bit blown.  So I double check my new connections, and everything is alright.<P>So I disconnect the 4x10s to hear if the 4.6's are doing it too.  Nope, they still sound great.<P>The only thing that is different between the 4x10's and the 4x6's is that there is a bunch of metal shavings stuck to all the magnets on the 4x10's, as a result of them not being in their box while I was grinding in my trunk today (see topic: <A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum1/HTML/006385.html)" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum1/HTML/006385.html)</A> <P>I tried to get all the crap off the magnets, but it's really hard to get them all, they just seem to attract more.<P>Is this the culprit to my crackling?  If so, is there a way to clean them up and get rid of all the metal?<P>TIA!<P>------------------<BR>AIM: Snowboarder0311<BR>87 Coupe 5sp. - big resto in progress<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/profile_1.JPG"> ||||reg||4
Z||000001||Fierowrecker||03-25-2001||09:50 AM||||Are ya sittin down?<BR>The 4X10's are prone to crackle at the bass freqs...<BR>That is why people put in round speakers in the front...<BR>My new 4X10's do the same, I just back off the bass for now, and I am working on an adaptor to fit bigger speakers under the dash...<BR>Possibly, two 5 inch round side by side?<BR>I saw a 6X9 installed there, but I like round speakers for clarity...<BR>crash...||||reg||1
Z||000002||KRMFiero||03-25-2001||02:25 PM||||6x9's hit alot harder then you would think, could you take your speaker back and pick up some 6x9's?<P>Kyle||||reg||1
Z||000003||sqoach||03-25-2001||02:42 PM||||only thing i could think is that the connections are mixed up. exactly what kind of "crackling" sound? i have a set of 4x10s in the dash and they sound great. they make some pretty good mid-bass. i don't see what the deal is with round being better than oval.<P>joe, if you have a local radio shack or something nearby, i would take it there and have them check it out. you can usually find someone nice enough to look at it for you. or you can at least ask them about it. or you can even talk to whoever you bought them from.<P>------------------<BR>sqoach@yahoo.com<P>       <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/sqoach.JPG">  <BR>84 SE (project car?)<BR>85 Coupe (parts car)<BR>86 SC (daily driver)||||reg||1
Z||000004||Oreif||03-25-2001||03:17 PM||||Are you sure it's the speakers? The grill covers have been known to vibrate and make noise. Also if you were grinding, you could have gotten metal shavings in the voice coil itself. If that's the case they may be trash.<BR>What brand of 4X10's did you get? I use to have Pioneers and the 4X6 would distort way before the 4X10's.||||reg||1
Z||000005||fyerstarter||03-25-2001||04:02 PM||||Since you have a sub now there is really no reason to have low frequencies going to your 4x10's. First you can test to see if they sound good at high frequencies by disconnecting all the speakers (or setting the balance and fader so that only he 4x10's are active) and adjusting your base all the way down. If they sound good then you can get a crossover (this will be a bit expensive) and only run mid to high freqnecies to those speakers or you can get capacitors from your local stereo shop that go in the positive wire which will filter out all the base (these are cheap).||||reg||1
Z||000006||FieroJoe||03-25-2001||06:25 PM||||They are pinoeer 4x10's, 120 watt max.  I bought them brand new at best buy last summer, and have sounded very good until just this incident.<P>Oreif - there is no dash in the car, they are just sitting there, or in my hand.  The connections have not changed, all I did was splice in the wire for the amp.  The highs sound ok, so I dont think any crap got in there.  But the lows are trash, intermittently.  Sometimes it is clean. others not,  I will go out and check the connections again now.<P>They sounded really good bass wise when the car was put back together.  I was surprised at how much bass they put out, for being so small.<P>------------------<BR>AIM: Snowboarder0311<BR>87 Coupe 5sp. - big resto in progress<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/profile_1.JPG"> ||||reg||1
Z||000007||FieroJoe||03-25-2001||07:24 PM||||well, I just went out and listened to it for a while.  The 4x10s did their crackly thing on and off.  If I moved the cone by hand, I felt some grinding  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/eek.gif"> but the crackling would stop.  I just sat and played around with all the settings, and it seemed to be back to normal.  I hope they stay that way!<P>I am very impressed with the performance sound system.  I never expected that much bass from just a 5" speaker.  I dont think i could have built a better sub system for the $125 that I paid for it.  And the overhead slider is just a big bonus, I love it!<P>------------------<BR>AIM: Snowboarder0311<BR>87 Coupe 5sp. - big resto in progress<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/profile_1.JPG"> ||||reg||1
